Troubleshooting
Symptom: mvn compile fails with "package org.apache.camel does not exist". Diagnostic hint: check that the pom.xml was saved with the exact camel.version property and dependency coordinates shown in Subtask 1.2, and confirm Maven can reach its remote repository (run mvn -o compile to check if you are accidentally in offline mode).
Symptom: integration test testTransformRoute intermittently fails with "message count mismatch". Diagnostic hint: the timer route also sends messages into direct:transform and therefore into the intercepted direct:output; check whether the timer fired during your test run and consider excluding it or lowering repeatCount when running tests interactively.
Conclusion
In this lab, you built a complete Apache Camel project from scratch and implemented two custom processors by hand, coding the Processor interface directly rather than relying on built-in transformations. You learned how to insert processors into routes using the fluent .process() DSL method, how to chain multiple processors to compose more complex transformations, and how headers set by one processor become available to the next stage of the route. You also validated this behavior with both JUnit unit tests against a processor in isolation and Camel integration tests using CamelTestSupport and mock endpoints. Going forward, consider exploring Camel's built-in Enterprise Integration Pattern components (such as content-based routers and aggregators) to see where a custom processor is the right tool versus where a standard Camel construct would be simpler and more maintainable.
